## 8) Edge cases + test plan

### 8.1 Edge cases

| Edge case | Why it matters | Expected behavior |
|---|---|---|
| Last packet smaller than payload size | Needed for correct file reconstruction | Receiver writes exact final byte count |
| Duplicate DATA packet after timeout | Common in stop-and-wait retransmission | Receiver does not write duplicate payload and re-ACKs prior success |
| Duplicate ACK packet | Can happen after retransmissions | Sender ignores ACKs for the wrong sequence |
| Corrupted ACK header or payload | Core Phase 3 corruption case | Sender rejects ACK and retransmits after timeout |
| Corrupted DATA packet | Must not write invalid bytes | Receiver drops packet and sends duplicate ACK |
| Lost ACK packet | Main new Phase 3 feature | Sender times out and retransmits |
| Lost DATA packet | Main new Phase 3 feature | Receiver never sees it, so sender times out and retransmits |
| EOF packet lost or ACKed late | Important for clean termination | Sender keeps retransmitting EOF until properly ACKed |
| Short malformed packet | Parser robustness | Receiver catches exception and sends duplicate ACK if possible |

### 8.2 Tests

**Unit-style checks**
- Checksum correctness on known byte sequences
- Packet encode/decode round-trip consistency
- Corruption helper flips exactly one bit
- EOF packet generation produces a zero-length DATA packet with EOF flag

**Integration tests**
- Send a file at 0% loss/error and verify the output hash matches the input hash
- Run with ACK corruption enabled and verify successful transfer
- Run with DATA corruption enabled and verify successful transfer
- Run with ACK loss enabled and verify timeout-driven retransmission
- Run with DATA loss enabled and verify timeout-driven retransmission
- Verify final output file size matches original input size
- Verify repeated runs with fixed seed produce stable results

